Article 20

(1) Forms of services provided to women and child victims of violence, are as follows:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal assistance;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. psychological services; and
g. economic empowerment.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial governments, other regency/city governments, communities, families and parents.
(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for services and handling of women and child victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 12

(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 10 letter b, are implemented in the form of community participation.

(2) Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:

a. preventing acts of Violence against victims;
b. establishing complaint posts at the subdistrict/village level;
c. establishing women and children protection units within community organizations;
d. independently conducting socialization of women's rights and children's rights;
e. providing first aid to victims; and
f. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector, and mass media.
(4) Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:

a. forms of violence;
b. rights of victims;
c. obligations and responsibilities;
d. implementation of protection;
e. guidance and supervision;
f. reporting; and
g. funding;

Article 15

(1) In providing Protection for Women and Children Victims

of Violence, the Integrated Service Center is obliged to:

a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to Victims;
b. organize Protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to Medical Rehabilitation, Social Rehabilitation, Repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for Victims;
d. network with government or private hospitals for the care and recovery of Victims' health and cooperate with witness and Victim protection institutions, social Protection homes or government, community, or other institutions' trauma centers for the recovery of Victims' health; provide convenience, comfort, and safety for Victims;
f. maintain the confidentiality of Victims;
g. provide economic empowerment;
h. provide fulfillment of legal assistance for Victims;
i. in the event that integrated service implementation is carried out in a networked manner, the Integrated Service Center remains responsible for the entire referral process of services required by Victims.
(2) The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented in accordance with Minimum Service Standards.

Article 24

(1) The Regional

Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence.

(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:

a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and
e. evaluation.
(3) Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be carried out with the principles of:
a. professional;
b. transparent; and
c. accountable.

Article 7

Neglect as referred to in Article 3 letter d is caused by:

a. acts that result in the non-fulfillment of the needs of children properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after children as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting persons within the scope of the household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or by reason of an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to such persons; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by limiting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of such person.

